Define Euclidean finite-action boundary data and the relation to compactification or a clutching function before defining charge. State the orientation, \(*\)-operator, structure group and invariant polynomial, curvature convention, Chern–Weil normalization, and whether the result is a real characteristic number or an integer. The current Chern–Weil theorem reaches real de Rham classes; it is not an integral-lattice theorem for arbitrary \(G\).

For a declared \(SU(2)\) or \(SU(N)\) bundle use the chosen associated bundle and its \(c_2\) convention. Prove the normalized topological charge formula and quantization from the integral characteristic class/classification assumptions; do not import \(\operatorname{tr}_{\mathrm{fund}}\) normalization without deriving the coefficient. Prove the Euclidean action inequality by decomposing \(F=F^++F^-\), identify equality with \(F=\pm *F\), then derive the classical Yang–Mills equation from Bianchi. The existence and uniqueness of a charge-one BPST representative is a separate source-backed statement: a local worked example verifies one formula but does not classify all charge-one solutions.

Any semiclassical instanton contribution to a path integral additionally needs gauge zero-mode handling, the fluctuation determinant and its regulator/renormalization, collective-coordinate measure, ghost determinant, and any fermion zero modes and selection rules. If these are only a saddle expansion, label it formal/asymptotic and state the expansion parameter and domain. An instanton is a classical Euclidean solution; it is not a proof of the mass gap or confinement.

### Lattice regulators, Wilson lines, and confinement

For a new non-Abelian lattice model, define a finite cell complex or graph, \(G\)-valued edge variables, vertex gauge action, ordered plaquette holonomy, Haar integration or the Hamiltonian link Hilbert space \(L^2(G)\), the exact gauge-invariant action/transfer matrix, and Gauss operators. Prove the finite-regulator gauge invariance and physical subspace separately. The imported compact \(U(1)\) rotor/CAR system is a useful analogy but is not a non-Abelian link model.

Define a Wilson loop by the trace in a specified representation of the path-ordered continuum holonomy or ordered lattice edge product; prove gauge invariance by conjugation at the basepoint. A confinement criterion must say whether it is a pure-gauge center-symmetry statement, an area law for representations of nonzero \(N\)-ality, a static-source potential, or absence of colored asymptotic states. With dynamical fundamental matter, a long string may break and a Wilson area law is not a universal diagnostic. Define the infinite-loop/large-distance and continuum/infinite-volume limits, including their order.

A strong-coupling area-law proof on a compact lattice is valuable and may be a theorem in its stated coupling regime. It must give an exact convergent expansion or inequalities and cannot be promoted to the asymptotically-free continuum theory without a separate continuum-limit proof. Wilson’s 1974 paper itself states that the computable strong-coupling limit loses Lorentz and Euclidean invariance. Lattice evidence, continuum-extrapolated simulations, effective models, and a mathematical construction have different evidential status.

## Frontier and open-problem status

The one proposed open item is **op-qftx-clay-4d-yang-mills-existence-mass-gap**, classified as mathematics and nonlogical. The official Jaffe–Witten problem states: for every compact simple gauge group \(G\), prove existence of a nontrivial quantum Yang–Mills theory on \(\mathbb R^4\) with a positive mass gap \(\Delta>0\), with existence axioms at least as strong as Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader. In that source, a mass gap means the Hamiltonian has no spectrum in \((0,\Delta)\) above the vacuum. The current Clay “Unsolved” archive still lists “Yang-Mills & the Mass Gap,” checked 2026-10-23. The item’s Status should preserve that date/source, cite partial finite-lattice/other-dimensional/regulator results without overgeneralizing, and state what remains required. It is not a dependency or a premise.

The official Clay formulation does not ask for a proof of the Wilson-loop area law or color confinement. Jaffe–Witten discuss mass gap, confinement, and chiral symmetry breaking as separate desired quantum properties, then call confinement a natural extension of the prize problem. Keep these formulations separate. This scaffold does not create an op-item for “confinement” until the exact observable, group, matter content, limit, and current status have their own source review. A theorem awaiting local proof (classical Yang–Mills variation, BPST verification, local slice theorem, finite-lattice strong-coupling expansion) remains an authoring obligation and must not be mislabeled open.

The following statuses are appropriate at this research stage:

- Classical principal-bundle identities and the charge-one BPST solution are established mathematics/physics results; the new items still owe local proofs in this library.
- The Singer global-gauge-fixing obstruction is an established result with exact topological hypotheses; only its abstract was available in this review, so its full theorem statement and proof route must be checked before authoring.
- Asymptotic freedom and perturbative renormalization are established within specified perturbative schemes; local non-Abelian derivations and conditions still need to be authored. Neither establishes a nonperturbative continuum limit.
- Compact-lattice strong-coupling binding/area-law results are model/regime results. Do not use them as the continuum Yang–Mills mass-gap theorem.
- The Clay four-dimensional existence/mass-gap problem remains officially unsolved as checked 2026-10-23.

## Source ledger: exact claims and reading limits

1. **Matthijs Vákár, “Principal Bundles and Gauge Theories,” arXiv:2110.06334 (2021).** Direct targeted reading of the HTML for §1.3.1, §2.1 and §2.6.5 (not the whole thesis). §1.3.1 defines principal connection one-forms through horizontal projection and the fundamental vertical action, gives the Cartan curvature \(d\omega+\tfrac12[\omega,\omega]\), and relates principal connection equivariance to the adjoint action. §2.1 defines a gauge transformation as a principal automorphism over the identity and notes the local \(M\to G\) description in a trivialization. §2.6.5 varies the gauge-field action with compactly supported variations on relatively compact open sets and obtains the covariant Yang–Mills equation. The thesis is a mathematical exposition and includes a classical gauge-field action; it does not construct a quantum Yang–Mills theory, prove a path-integral identity, or replace this category’s local proof obligations. The imported root principal-connection items cite the corresponding printed pages 32–36 and 53–54.

2. **Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang–Mills Theory,” official Clay problem description (2000).** Read the Clay-hosted 14-page PDF, especially §§1–2, §4 “The Problem,” and §5–6. It gives the compact-group classical curvature/action conventions, distinguishes the three quantum properties mass gap, confinement and chiral symmetry breaking, defines the mass-gap spectral condition, and states the exact compact-simple-\(G\), \(\mathbb R^4\) existence-and-gap problem. Its problem text describes mathematical construction as incomplete; the separate current Clay “Unsolved Archives” page was checked 2026-10-23. This is the authoritative problem formulation/status source, not a proof that any physical model has the stated properties.

3. **Glenn Barnich, Friedemann Brandt, Marc Henneaux, “Local BRST cohomology in gauge theories,” Physics Reports 338 (2000), arXiv:hep-th/0002245.** Targeted reading of the abstract, §2.2 (Yang–Mills-type field content and BRST transformations), §2.3 (nilpotence, \(H(s)\), \(H(s\mid d)\)), §2.7 (contractible nonminimal pairs), §11 (the stated cohomology theorem for Yang–Mills-type theories without free Abelian gauge symmetries, in \(n>2\)), and §12.3 (ghost-number-one anomaly classes). The text defines a specific class of local theories: matter transforms linearly in a completely reducible representation; the Lagrangian is local and gauge invariant up to a divergence; additional matter gauge symmetries are excluded for that analysis. Its local anomaly equation is \(sa+dm=0\), with counterterms removing trivial classes. The review’s theorems have technical hypotheses and selected cases; this targeted reading is not a proof reconstruction or a blanket anomaly-cancellation theorem for any gauge/matter model. The new items must reproduce the exact chosen subcase and proof locally.

4. **I. M. Singer, “Some Remarks on the Gribov Ambiguity,” Communications in Mathematical Physics 60 (1978), 7–12, DOI 10.1007/BF01609471.** The Project Euclid paper endpoint returned an anti-bot interstitial, so only its authoritative abstract/catalogue and first introductory lines were available. The abstract studies connections of a principal bundle over \(S^4\) with compact non-Abelian Lie group and states that no continuous choice of exactly one connection per orbit is possible. No details of the proof, topology on the configuration/orbit spaces, or treatment of stabilizers are imported from this read. Retrieve/read pp. 7–12 and specify those hypotheses before using it as an established theorem in active content.

5. **A. A. Belavin, A. M. Polyakov, A. S. Schwartz, Yu. S. Tyupkin, “Pseudoparticle Solutions of the Yang–Mills Equations,” Physics Letters B 59 (1975), 85–87.** The full three-page primary paper was retrieved and read. In the authors’ Euclidean four-dimensional conventions, it constructs regular finite-action self-dual SU(2) charge-one pseudoparticles, identifies the charge from the asymptotic \(S^3\to SU(2)\) map, and shows that the self-dual solution saturates the action lower bound in that normalization. The paper does not provide a general moduli-space theorem and explicitly says its authors did not know whether charge \(q>1\) self-dual solutions exist at that time; do not recycle that historical uncertainty as a current open status. Translate signs and trace normalization into the library’s declared \(c_2\)/Chern–Weil convention.

6. **Kenneth G. Wilson, “Confinement of Quarks,” Physical Review D 10 (1974), 2445–2459.** Read the APS abstract/metadata only, not the 15-page article. The abstract says the lattice gauge theory has a computable strong-coupling limit in which Wilson’s binding mechanism applies and free quarks are absent, while that limit lacks Lorentz and Euclidean invariance. It is cited only as a historically important regulated strong-coupling source; do not attribute a fully specified area-law proof or continuum result to the abstract.

7. **David J. Gross and Frank Wilczek, “Ultraviolet Behavior of Non-Abelian Gauge Theories,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1343–1346; H. David Politzer, “Reliable Perturbative Results for Strong Interactions?,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1346–1349.** Read the APS abstracts only. Gross–Wilczek state that a wide class of non-Abelian gauge theories have free-field ultraviolet asymptotic behavior up to calculable logarithmic corrections. Politzer states a perturbative result for deep-Euclidean Green functions of Yang–Mills theories and specified fermionic extensions. The exact one-/higher-loop coefficients, scheme assumptions, and counterterms are not imported from the abstracts; derive or source them in the perturbative renormalization branch. These statements say nothing by themselves about the infrared or a convergent perturbation series.

8. **G. ’t Hooft and M. Veltman, “Regularization and Renormalization of Gauge Fields,” Nuclear Physics B 44 (1972), 189–213.** Bibliographic record and abstract inspected; the full paper was not read for this scaffold. It presents dimensional regularization/renormalization methods for gauge fields and Yang–Mills-type theories. Cite it as a source to study, not as the proof of the new Slavnov–Taylor or anomaly statements.

9. **V. N. Faddeev and V. N. Popov, “Feynman Diagrams for the Yang–Mills Field,” Physics Letters B 25 (1967), 29–30.** Metadata and abstract inspected; full text not read. The abstract refers to the extra loop contributions in Yang–Mills perturbation theory. The new local Faddeev–Popov identity must be derived from a stated slice/Jacobian setup; the source citation alone proves no local or global gauge-fixing theorem.

## Integration and review obligations

Before this design is made a canonical physics plan, the integrator should bind every proposed dependency to the current exact root import or to a documented planned math result with its statement, hypotheses, proof status, and consumer map. In particular: reconcile the root BV page’s item metadata/page-prose mismatch; reconcile the new root ASD items with the frozen physics import snapshot; do not treat a root draft or untracked file as a proof supplier; pin the structure group/global form and the invariant trace normalization; separate formal perturbation theory from physical assumptions and empirical evidence; retrieve Singer’s proof before stating its full theorem; and keep the Clay open problem nonlogical. A/B example pages, source status, provenance, physics scopes, item dependency roles, and axiom-base carry-through must follow physics/SCHEMA.md and physics/WORKFLOW.md. No category carrier, canonical plan, engine, or other run was changed by this note.

