+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\), a smooth right principal bundle \(P\to M\), a principal connection form \(\omega\), a smooth finite-dimensional complex representation \(\rho\), its associated vector bundle \(E\), and a smooth section \(\sigma\) of \(E\).
+
+[A1] The only inherited choice assumption is \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\), used in the supplied Lie-group tangent-bracket and horizontal-connection constructions. [[def-countable-choice]]
+
+[F1] The smooth right action has equivariant local principal charts and each point has unique group coordinates relative to a local section. [[def-smooth-principal-g-bundle]]
+
+[F2] The associated quotient relation is \([pg,v]=[p,\rho(g)v]\). [[def-associated-bundle-to-a-principal-bundle-and-representation]]
+
+[F3] The quotient has smooth vector-bundle charts induced by local sections; its coordinate transition is the representation applied to the principal transition element. [[thm-associated-vector-bundle-is-well-defined]]
+
+[F4] The principal form reproduces fundamental fields and obeys \(R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega\). [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]]
+
+[F5] The left Maurer–Cartan form is \((\theta_L)_g=d(L_{g^{-1}})_g\), so \(\theta_L(g)(X)=g^{-1}dg(X)\) in group notation. [[def-left-maurer-cartan-form]]
+
+[F6] Differentials are linear and satisfy the chain rule. [[def-differential-of-a-smooth-map]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-differentials-of-smooth-maps]]
+
+[F7] For fixed \(g\in G\), the adjoint map is \(\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e\), where \(C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}\). [[def-conjugation-and-the-adjoint-representation-of-a-lie-group]]
+
+[F8] The curve \(t\mapsto\exp_G(tZ)\) has initial tangent \(Z\) at the identity. [[def-exponential-map-of-a-lie-group]], [[thm-one-parameter-subgroups-are-integral-curves-of-left-invariant-fields]]
+
+[F9] The representation \(\rho\) is a group homomorphism, so \(\rho(gh)=\rho(g)\rho(h)\) and \(\rho(e)=\operatorname{id}_V\). [[def-finite-dimensional-representation-of-a-group-over-a-field]]
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 On a principal chart, each \(p\in\pi^{-1}(U_i)\) has a unique expression \(p=s_i(x)h\). The associated-bundle chart therefore assigns \([s_i(x)h,v]\) the vector coordinate \(\rho(h)v\). In particular \([s_i(x),\psi_i(x)]\) determines a unique smooth component \(\psi_i\). If \(s_j=s_i g_{ij}\), then \([s_j,\psi_j]=[s_i,\rho(g_{ij})\psi_j]\); equality with \([s_i,\psi_i]\) gives \(\psi_j=\rho(g_{ij}^{-1})\psi_i\). [F1, F2, F3, given]
+
+1.2 In a finite basis of \(V\), \(\operatorname{GL}_{\mathbb C}(V)\) is the open subset where the determinant is nonzero, so its tangent space at the identity is \(\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(V)\). Thus the differential \(\rho_*\) is real-linear by [F6]. For fixed \(g\), [F9] gives \(\rho\circ C_{g^{-1}}=C_{\rho(g^{-1})}\circ\rho\). The derivative of target conjugation is the linear map \(T\mapsto\rho(g^{-1})T\rho(g)\), while the derivative of source conjugation is \(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\) by [F7]. The chain rule [F6] therefore gives \(\rho_*(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Z)=\rho(g^{-1})\rho_*(Z)\rho(g)\). For a smooth transition \(g_{ij}:U_i\cap U_j\to G\), differentiation of \(s_j=s_i g_{ij}\) decomposes a tangent vector into the right translate of the tangent from \(s_i\) and the fundamental vertical vector with generator \(\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)\), by [F5]. Reproduction and equivariance in [F4] yield \(A_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+\theta_L(g_{ij})\). [A1, F1, F4, F5, F6, F7, F9, step 1.1]
+
+2.1 Put \(g=g_{ij}(x)\), \(Z=\theta_L(g)(X)\), and \(R=\rho(g^{-1})\). Along a curve through \(x\) tangent to \(X\), the transition curve has initial tangent \(d(L_g)_eZ\), by [F5]. The curve \(t\mapsto g\exp_G(tZ)\) has the same initial value and tangent, so the differentials of smooth composites along these curves agree by [F6]. Its inverse is exactly \(\exp_G(-tZ)g^{-1}\); [F8] and the homomorphism law [F9] therefore give \(dR(X)=-\rho_*(Z)R\). Using steps 1.1 and 1.2, \(d\psi_j(X)+\rho_*(A_j(X))\psi_j=-\rho_*(Z)R\psi_i+R\,d\psi_i(X)+\rho_*(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}A_i(X))R\psi_i+\rho_*(Z)R\psi_i=R\bigl(d\psi_i(X)+\rho_*(A_i(X))\psi_i\bigr)\), where the first and last \(Z\)-terms cancel and the adjoint-intertwining identity from step 1.2 gives the final equality. This is precisely the associated-bundle coordinate transition for an \(E\)-valued 1-form, so the local expressions define one global smooth \(E\)-valued 1-form and do not depend on the selected sections. [F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F8, F9, step 1.1, step 1.2]
+
+3.1 In any local chart, multiplication by a scalar function obeys \(d(f\psi_i)=df\otimes\psi_i+f\,d\psi_i\); the endomorphism term obeys \(\rho_*(A_i)(f\psi_i)=f\rho_*(A_i)\psi_i\) by pointwise linearity. Adding these identities gives \((\nabla^\omega(f\sigma))_i=df\otimes\psi_i+f(\nabla^\omega\sigma)_i\), which is the asserted Leibniz rule in the associated bundle. If \(V=0\), every term is zero and the same formulas hold; the argument is pointwise and also applies on an empty base. [F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+For clarity, the square below describes how the local covariant-derivative formula is transported between two local sections. The top arrow changes the section and its field components together; the lower arrow is the associated vector-bundle coordinate change. Step 2.1 proves the square commutes, and the proof does not rely on the diagram.
+
+```tikzcd
+\{\psi_i\in C^\infty(U_{ij},V)\} \arrow[r,"\psi_i\mapsto\rho(g_{ij}^{-1})\psi_i"] \arrow[d,"\psi\mapsto d\psi+\rho_*(A_i)\psi"'] & \{\psi_j\in C^\infty(U_{ij},V)\} \arrow[d,"\psi\mapsto d\psi+\rho_*(A_j)\psi"] \\
+\Omega^1(U_{ij},V) \arrow[r,"\alpha\mapsto\rho(g_{ij}^{-1})\alpha"'] & \Omega^1(U_{ij},V)
+```
