+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** A smooth manifold $M$, a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$, and homogeneous forms $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ of degrees $p,q,r$.
+
+[F1] A finite-dimensional real Lie algebra has a bilinear alternating bracket satisfying Jacobi. [[def-finite-dimensional-lie-algebra]]
+
+[F2] A smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued form has smooth scalar components in any finite basis, and its exterior derivative is taken componentwise. [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]]
+
+[F3] The scalar exterior derivative is a graded derivation of the wedge product. [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-is-a-graded-derivation]]
+
+[F4] Differential forms form an associative graded-commutative algebra. [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]], [[prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra]]
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Choose a finite basis $(e_a)_{a=1}^n$ of $\mathfrak g$ with dual basis $(e^a)_{a=1}^n$, and write $\alpha=\sum_a e_a\alpha^a$, $\beta=\sum_b e_b\beta^b$, where $\alpha^a=e^a\circ\alpha$ and $\beta^b=e^b\circ\beta$ are smooth scalar forms. Expanding the alternating permutation sum gives $[\alpha\wedge\beta]=\sum_{a,b}[e_a,e_b]\,\alpha^a\wedge\beta^b$; this finite sum is smooth and alternating, hence is in $\Omega^{p+q}(M,\mathfrak g)$. [F1, F2, F4, construct]
+
+1.2 Swapping the two blocks of $p$ and $q$ arguments in the defining permutation sum contributes the sign $(-1)^{pq}$; antisymmetry of the inner Lie bracket contributes the minus sign. Therefore $[\alpha\wedge\beta]=-(-1)^{pq}[\beta\wedge\alpha]$. [F1, F4, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 In the finite basis expansion of step 1.1, apply the scalar graded derivation rule [F3] to each $\alpha^a\wedge\beta^b$ and retain the fixed coefficient $[e_a,e_b]$. Summing the resulting finite terms gives $d[\alpha\wedge\beta]=[d\alpha\wedge\beta]+(-1)^p[\alpha\wedge d\beta]$. [F2, F3, step 1.1]
+
+3.1 For homogeneous $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$, the same basis expansion writes $\left[\alpha\wedge\left[\beta\wedge\gamma\right]\right]$ with coefficient $[e_a,[e_b,e_c]]$ and form factor $\alpha^a\wedge\beta^b\wedge\gamma^c$. The right-hand terms have coefficients $\left[[e_a,e_b],e_c\right]$ and, after commuting $\beta^b$ past $\alpha^a$ using [F4], $[e_b,[e_a,e_c]]$, with the same form factor. The equality of coefficients is exactly the Lie-algebra Jacobi identity in [F1], so the graded Jacobi formula follows. [F1, F4, step 1.1]
+
+4.1 If $\mathfrak g=0$, all forms and brackets are zero; if $p=0$ or $q=0$, the same formulas use the empty wedge with sign $+1$. The finite basis in step 1.1 is one finite-dimensional coordinate calculation, not a choice from a family. The definition and identities hold on the empty manifold as well. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
