+## Source reading and harvested-result ledger
+
+The full relevant reasoning in both independent treatments was inspected directly. No source retrieval failed, so no recovery retry or `source_resolution` disposition is needed. The local proofs below do not cite a source as a substitute for an argument.
+
+1. **Matthijs Vákár, “Principal Bundles and Gauge Theories,” arXiv:2110.06334** — [full HTML](https://arxiv.org/html/2110.06334). Read §§1.1.3, 1.1.5, 1.3.1, 1.3.3, 1.4.1, and 2.1 in full. §1.1.3/1.1.5 supports the right-principal/associated quotient conventions (`def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data`); §1.3.1 proves the orbit differential isomorphism, connection-form reproduction/equivariance, Cartan formula and Bianchi identity (`def-qftx-principal-connection-form`, `def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket`, `lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi`); §§1.3.3/1.4.1 support induced associated connections and local covariant-derivative form (`def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative`); §2.1 distinguishes bundle automorphisms from local functions into the group. Disposition: all four assigned claims are independently proved locally; the source is supporting context, not a logical proof supplier.
+
+2. **Karl-Hermann Neeb, “Differential Topology of Fiber Bundles,” full lecture notes (2024)** — [full PDF](https://www.math.fau.de/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/bundle-lect.pdf). Read Chapter 1 §§1.4, 1.6; Chapter 5 §5.4; and Chapter 6 §6.1 in full. These sections support smooth principal/associated bundles, connection 1-form axioms and recovery of the horizontal distribution, the right-action transition law, curvature horizontality/equivariance, and the local Cartan expression (`def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data`, `def-qftx-principal-connection-form`, `def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket`, `lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi`, `def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative`). Disposition: local arguments and exact conventions are reproduced and checked in the item proofs; no quantum, physical, or path-integral claim is taken from the source.
