'ex-qftx-su2-noncommuting-curvature':r'''1.1 Regard "'$M_2('"\\mathbb C)"'$ as $'"\\mathbb R"'^8$. The conditions $g''^*g=I$ and $'"\\det g=1"'$ defining $SU(2)$ are closed equations in the matrix entries, so $SU(2)$ is closed. Each column of a unitary matrix has norm one, hence all entries have modulus at most one, so $SU(2)$ is bounded. By [F2], it is compact. The matrices $D='"\\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\\\0&-i\\end{pmatrix}"'$ and $J='"\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}"'$ are unitary with determinant one, but $DJ='"\\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\ne\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\-i&0\\end{pmatrix}=JD"'$. Thus $SU(2)$ is noncommutative, and [F1] supplies its Lie-group structure. This verifies the group conditions in [A1]. The declared axiom base is $'"\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega"'$ by [A2], while [F2] uses no choice. Each Pauli matrix is Hermitian and traceless, so each $T_a$ is anti-Hermitian and traceless; [F6] puts them in $'"\\mathfrak{su}(2)"'$. Direct multiplication gives $'"\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3"'$ and $'"\\sigma_2\\sigma_1=-i\\sigma_3"'$, hence $[T_1,T_2]=(-i/2)''^2['"\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2]=-(1/4)(2i\\sigma_3)=T_3"'$. Since $T_3'"\\ne0"'$, this also verifies that $T_1$ and $T_2$ do not commute. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F6, algebra]

